\section{Core Architectural Principles}

Telix synthesizes its architectural foundation around four primary
domain goals:

\begin{description}
    \item[Formal Verification:] Formally verify via \CoqRocq\ or other
	    theorem provers.

    \item[Hardware Partitioning and Safety Domain Isolation:] Systems
	    are partitioned per the Barrelfish multikernel model
	    \footfullcite{baum09} into decoupled message-passing nodes
	    (one kernel instance per NUMA node, rather than per core).
	    Within each multikernel partition, Telix selectively adapts
	    the \Asterinas\ \framekernel\ technique
	    \footfullcite{peng24}: thin, performance-critical server
	    layers are pulled into the main kernel address space and
	    protected by Rust's compile-time safety guarantees rather
	    than by hardware MMU isolation.  The remaining servers
	    retain their own address spaces, kept out of the
	    framekernel TCB.  This hybrid boundary is the most
	    experimental aspect of the design (see
	    \S\ref{ch:control-plane}); if it proves unsound, Telix
	    degrades gracefully to a pure microkernel with all servers
	    isolated.

    \item[Hybrid Continuation-Passing and Network-Structured Control
	    Plane:] For fully asynchronous operation with efficiency
	    within shared memory domains while also cluster-ready by
	    virtue of discipline maintained for IPC.

    \item[Metadata-Light Virtual Memory Management:] Virtual memory
	    overhead is constrained to sublinear scaling relative to
	    physical memory size. Telix combines superpaging
	    \footfullcite{nava02}, shared page tables
	    \footfullcite{ko03}, software-managed inverted page tables
	    \footfullcite{tall95, huck93}, and BSD/Linux
	    \pageclustering\ algorithms \footfullcite{baba79, dick01}.
	    Additionally, traditional page descriptor arrays are
	    eliminated by adapting main-memory database morsel-driven
	    allocation\footfullcite{kemp14}.

    \item[Hybrid Workload Co-Scheduling:] Thread execution uses $M:N$
	    threading via Scheduler Activations \footfullcite{ande92}
	    coupled with Earliest Eligible Virtual Deadline First
	    (EEVDF) proportional allocation \footfullcite{stoi95} and
	    parallel gang co-scheduling \footfullcite{oust82, feit92}.

    \item[Asynchronous Zero-Copy I/O and Compatibility:] Inter-process
	    communication and system invocations rely on lock-free fast
	    buffers (Fbufs) \footfullcite{drus93} and completion-driven
	    queues \footfullcite{krie06, ande91}. Multiple binary
	    compatibility interfaces (Linux, Windows) are supported
	    through a dynamic personality redirection subsystem
	    \footfullcite{gerb01, golu91, zajc93, rash87, milo93}.
\end{description}

\section{Target Hardware Architectures \& Execution Models}
\label{sec:target_architectures}

Telix is designed to target modern 64-bit multi-core hardware platforms.
Rather than tailoring kernel abstractions to a single ISA, Telix defines a
uniform execution model while accommodating architectural differences in MMU
topologies, memory consistency models, and hardware capability enforcement.

\begin{description}
    \item[RISC-V (\texttt{rv64g} / \texttt{rv64gc}):] Serving as the
	    primary open reference architecture. RISC-V provides clean
	    software-managed page table options (Sv39/Sv48),
	    standardized formal Sail ISA definitions
	    \footcite{armstrong19}, and an extensible ISA interface
	    well-suited for framekernel isolation domains. The Svnapot
	    extensions are of particular interest for the sake of
	    demonstrating Telix' abilities.

    \item[ARMv8-A / ARMv9-A (AArch64):] The primary production target
	    for high-core-count datacenter nodes and modern IoT devices.
	    Telix explicitly accounts for AArch64's weakly-ordered
	    memory model when synthesizing cross-core synchronization
	    ring buffers and page table updates \footcite{sosp21vrm}.

    \item[LoongArch:] Mandatory because China is the future.
    \item[MIPS:] The basic picture of sanity.

    \item[Capability Hardware (CHERI / Arm Morello):] Target platform
	    for high-assurance deployments. Hardware fine-grained
	    capabilities allow Telix to enforce spatial and temporal
	    memory safety directly in hardware, complementing Rust's
	    software safety guarantees within framekernels
	    \footcite{katamaran23}. The MIPS \texttt{PageGrain}
	    extension is of particular interest for the sake of
	    demonstrating Telix' abilities.

    \item[x86-64:] Supported primarily for broad compatibility on
	    commodity hardware. While x86-64 provides Total Store Order
	    (TSO) memory semantics simplifying ring buffer mechanics,
	    its rigid multi-level page table hierarchy demands shared
	    page table tree strategies \footcite{ko03}. Its segregated
	    TLBs also defeat much of the purpose of superpaging.
\end{description}

